"# Chapter 17 — Libs and modules: introspecting the loader

The registry (Chapter 11) told you every verb that exists right now. This chapter goes one level up: WHERE do those verbs come from? Which library shipped `chem/molar-mass`? Which libraries has your session actually installed vs. which are just available? How do you force a lazy library to load NOW so it doesn't stutter your next tick?

Introspection at the loader level is what makes the difference between a language that has libraries and a language that KNOWS ABOUT its libraries. Motoi knows.

## 17.1 Three verbs

Motoi installs three loader-introspection verbs at boot. All three are pure — they don't mutate anything; they just report.

```scheme
(require lib-name-symbol)  ; install a lazy library RIGHT NOW
(loaded-libs)              ; every library that has actually installed so far
(available-libs)           ; every library the manifest knows about
```

That's the whole surface. Together they let you see what's loaded, what's available but unloaded, and force a load when you need one.

## 17.2 available-libs — what could load

At boot Motoi reads its manifest — the list of every library file the runtime knows about — and remembers each one's name. Some install immediately (eager), some wait for their first call (lazy). Either way, `available-libs` returns the full list:

```scheme
(available-libs)
;; => (ai/ai ai/steering audio/tick base/alg base/assert
;;     book/reader book/tutor game/entity game/game
;;     ... 55 or so libs ...)
```

This is your map of the language. Every symbol in the list is a library that Motoi's manifest promises to load — either it already has, or it will as soon as you touch one of its verbs.

## 17.3 loaded-libs — what has actually loaded

`loaded-libs` reports a subset — only the libraries that have actually run their install function this session.

```scheme
(loaded-libs)
;; => ()   ; a fresh session hasn't triggered any lazy libs yet
```

Now touch a lazy verb:

```scheme
(assert/check-with positive? 5 \"must be positive\")   ; installs base/assert on first call
(loaded-libs)
;; => (base/assert)
```

The library was in `available-libs` from boot. The first call to `assert/check-with` triggered its install. Now it's in `loaded-libs` too.

Eager libraries — the ones that install at boot regardless — don't appear in `loaded-libs`. They're just always there; `loaded-libs` is specifically the lazy audit trail.

## 17.4 require — install a library on purpose

Sometimes you don't want to wait for the first-call surprise. You know your game loop will use `matrix/*` and `num-integrate/*` — you want those installed BEFORE the tick starts, not on frame 47 when a physics bug fires them for the first time.

`require` installs a lazy library synchronously:

```scheme
(require 'matrix)          ; install lib/math/matrix.js right now, block until done
;; => ok
```

Return values:

- `'ok` — the library was in the manifest and its installer ran (or it was already installed and this call was a no-op).
- `'unknown` — no library in the manifest matched that name.

`require` matches by name — `'assert` finds `base/assert`, `'matrix` finds `math/matrix`, `'game-theory` finds `game/game-theory`. If two libraries share a trailing name (shouldn't happen in a healthy manifest), the first match wins.

## 17.5 The idiom — warm up in setup, run in the loop

Games and animations want zero surprises in the tick. Force loads at setup:

```scheme
(define (setup)
  (require 'matrix)
  (require 'num-integrate)
  ;; ... any other lazy libs the tick loop will touch
  'ready)

(define (tick dt)
  ;; No first-touch stalls here — everything's already installed.
  ...)

(setup)
;; ... start the tick loop ...
```

Scratchpads and REPL sessions don't need this discipline. Reach for what you want; let the first-call install fire; move on.

## 17.6 What the numbers look like

A representative Motoi boot on a modern machine:

- `installCoreLazy` boot (eager + lazy manifest): ~0.4 ms
- First call to a lazy verb (installs the lib): ~0.4 ms
- Subsequent calls to that verb: sub-microsecond dispatch

If your program is a game loop hitting 60 fps (16 ms per frame budget), a 0.4 ms first-touch on frame 47 is a 2.5% budget spike for one frame. Usually invisible. Sometimes visible. For guaranteed silky, force the loads at setup.

## 17.7 Why not always eager?

Because expressiveness matters. If Motoi forced every one of its 900+ verbs to install at boot, cold startup would be 10× slower and every REPL session would pay for libraries it never touches. Lazy loading means the runtime charges you only for what you use.

But you can override that on a per-library basis — every library file declares its own `LAZY` flag. Change it, save, run. If you're shipping a game, flip everything the game touches to eager. If you're writing a notebook, leave things lazy.

See `docs/EAGER-VS-LAZY.md` (or the engineering doctrine chapter) for the full framing.

## 17.8 A loader-introspection pattern

Here's a small script that reports what your session has loaded so far and how much of the manifest is still cold:

```scheme
(define (loader-report)
  (let* ((all (available-libs))
         (loaded (loaded-libs))
         (loaded-count (length loaded))
         (all-count (length all)))
    (list
      :available all-count
      :loaded loaded-count
      :cold (- all-count loaded-count))))

(loader-report)
;; => (:available 55 :loaded 3 :cold 52)
```

Useful in a debug HUD, a REPL diagnostic verb, or the tail of a test run to prove the test only touched what it claimed to touch.

## 17.9 The whole introspection ladder

The introspection surface stacks:

- Chapter 3: `describe`, `type-of` — one value at a time
- Chapter 4: `doc-of`, `source-of` — one verb's metadata
- Chapter 5: `all-verbs`, environments — one env at a time
- Chapter 11: `snapshot-registry` — every verb, one map
- **Chapter 17 (this): `available-libs`, `loaded-libs`, `require` — every library, plus lazy control**

Each layer looks at a bigger unit of the runtime. The last one — libraries — is the outermost layer before you're looking at the file system itself.

## Applies to Motoi

A sister dialect is a variant of Motoi. It inherits the loader and these three verbs. If you're reading a sister dialect's Book of Introspection and see the same chapter, the semantics are identical — the manifest content will differ (its libraries are different), but the introspection API is the same."

  :verbs (
    (:name "require"
     :signature "(require lib-name) -> ok | unknown"
     :example "(require 'matrix)")
    (:name "loaded-libs"
     :signature "(loaded-libs) -> list"
     :example "(loaded-libs) ; => (base/assert)")
    (:name "available-libs"
     :signature "(available-libs) -> list"
     :example "(available-libs) ; => (ai/ai audio/tick ...)"))
  :epilogue null)
